.TH BUSYBOX 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
busybox \- one program providing the shell and many Unix commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I applet
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B busybox
.RI [ applet
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B busybox
.BR \-\-list [ \-full ]
.br
.B busybox \-\-install
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-u
|
.IR dir ]
.br
.B busybox \-\-uninstall
.RB [ \-n ]
.I file
.br
.B busybox \-\-help
.RI [ applet ]
.br
.B busybox \-\-version
.br
.B busybox \-\-busybox
.I applet
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B busybox
is a single program,
.IR busybox.exe ,
that contains the kit's shell and most of its Unix commands, each called
an applet.
It is busybox-w32, the Windows port of BusyBox, with w64devkit's
patches; see
.BR NOTES .
An applet is a small implementation of the command of the same name,
usually with fewer options than its GNU or BSD namesake.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B busybox
prints its version, a usage summary, and the list of applets on
standard output, and exits.
.PP
Every applet except
.BR [ ,
.BR echo ,
.BR false ,
.BR test ,
and
.B true
prints its usage on standard output and exits with status 0 when its
first argument is
.BR \-\-help .
In the shell, a builtin of the same name, such as
.B printf
or
.BR kill ,
runs instead and does not do this.
.SS Choosing the applet
.B busybox
runs the applet named by its first command-line word, the name it was
started under.
The word is converted to lowercase, a leading
.B \-
is dropped, and so is a
.BR .exe ,
.BR .com ,
.BR .bat ,
.BR .cmd ,
or
.B .sh
suffix or a final period.
The last component of what remains names the applet, so that
.B LS.EXE
and
.B C:\ew64devkit\ebin\els.exe
both run
.BR ls .
.PP
A name that begins with
.BR busybox ,
such as
.B busybox.exe
or
.BR busybox64.exe ,
runs
.B busybox
itself, which performs one of the
.B OPTIONS
or runs the applet named by its first argument.
A directory part in that argument is ignored, but the rest must match
exactly:
.B busybox ls
works, but
.B busybox LS
and
.B busybox ls.exe
do not.
.PP
An unknown applet prints
.IB name ": applet not found"
on standard error, and the exit status is 127.
.SS Launchers
The kit's
.I bin
directory holds
.I busybox.exe
and, for most applets, a launcher named after the applet, such as
.IR ls.exe .
A launcher is a small separate program, not a link.
It starts
.I busybox.exe
from its own directory with its own command line unchanged, waits for
it, and exits with its exit status.
The launcher itself ignores Ctrl+C, leaving it to
.BR busybox .
.PP
These applets have no launcher:
.BR [ ,
.BR cdrop ,
.BR drop ,
.BR flock ,
.BR pdrop ,
.BR sha384sum ,
and
.BR stty .
Run them as
.B busybox
.IR applet ,
or by name in the kit's shell, which runs applets without searching
.BR PATH .
.SS Applets
This build has these applets.
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
and
.B lash
are names of the same shell.
