.SS Running commands
When the shell, or an applet such as
.BR env ,
.BR find ,
.BR time ,
.BR timeout ,
or
.BR xargs ,
runs a command, it finds the command as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
A command named by an applet runs that applet, even when
.B PATH
has a program of the same name, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
says otherwise.
.IP \(bu 2
A command given as
.IR /bin/name ,
.IR /usr/bin/name ,
.IR /sbin/name ,
or
.IR /usr/sbin/name ,
when no such file exists, runs the applet
.IR name .
.IP \(bu 2
Other commands without a directory part are looked up in
.BR PATH .
Each candidate is tried as given and then with
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
appended, unless it already ends in one of these or in a period.
An empty element in
.B PATH
stands for the current directory.
When started from a Windows environment, as from
.BR cmd.exe ,
busybox drops a single trailing semicolon from
.BR PATH .
.IP \(bu 2
A file that begins with
.B #!\&
runs under the interpreter it names.
For an interpreter in
.IR /bin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
or
.IR /usr/sbin ,
busybox runs the applet of that name if there is one, or else that file
if it exists, or else the program of that name in
.BR PATH ,
so that
.B #!/bin/sh
and
.B #!/usr/bin/env
lines work.
When the shell runs the file, this search uses
.B PATH
as it was when the shell started, ignoring changes made in the shell.
A file whose name ends in
.B .sh
and that has no
.B #!\&
line runs under
.BR sh .
.SH OPTIONS
These options are recognized only as the first argument.
Except for
.BR \-\-busybox ,
they work only when
.B busybox
is started under its own name, where any other first argument is an
applet name.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the name of each applet on its own line, in sorted order.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-full
Like
.BR \-\-list ,
but precede each name with a field of 8 columns, holding
.BR NOFORK ,
.BR noexec ,
or nothing, and with the applet's conventional Unix directory, such as
.BR usr/bin/ .
The shell runs
.B NOFORK
applets within its own process.
Any longer argument that begins with
.B \-\-list
works the same.
.TP
\fB\-\-install\fR [\fB\-s\fR] [\fB\-u\fR | \fIdir\fR]
Create a hard link to
.I busybox.exe
for each applet, named after the applet with
.B .exe
appended, in
.IR dir ,
which is created if needed, or else in the directory that contains
.IR busybox.exe .
Existing files are left alone.
Other failures are reported, but do not change the exit status.
.RS
.TP
.B \-s
Create symbolic links instead.
Windows allows this only with administrator rights or in developer
mode.
.TP
.B \-u
Install into the directories
.IR /bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
and
.I /usr/sbin
of the system drive, creating them, putting each applet in the
directory that
.B \-\-list\-full
shows.
.RE
.IP
The kit's launchers make this unnecessary for most applets.
.TP
\fB\-\-uninstall\fR [\fB\-n\fR] \fIfile\fR
Delete every hard link to
.IR file ,
including
.I file
itself.
Given
.IR busybox.exe ,
or any link that
.B \-\-install
made, this deletes
.I busybox.exe
and all its links, except that Windows refuses to delete the last link
to a running program.
The kit's launchers are not links, so given a launcher, this deletes
only that launcher.
Failures are reported, but do not change the exit status.
On Windows XP, which lacks the API for finding links, it does nothing.
.RS
.TP
.B \-n
Print the full path of each link instead of deleting it.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR [\fIapplet\fR]
Print the usage of
.I applet
on standard output and exit with status 0.
An unknown
.I applet
fails as described above.
Without
.IR applet ,
or when it is
.BR busybox ,
print what
.B busybox
prints with no arguments.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version on standard output and exit.
When further arguments follow,
.B \-\-version
is taken as an applet name, and fails.
.TP
\fB\-\-busybox\fR \fIapplet\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Run
.I applet
whatever name
.B busybox
was started under, even one that does not begin with
.BR busybox .
It treats
.I applet
as the name it was started under, so the rules of
.B Choosing the applet
apply:
.B "\-\-busybox LS.EXE"
runs
.BR ls .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The
.B BB_
variables are specific to busybox-w32.
In the shell, export them: busybox reads them from the environment.
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialogs, such as for a drive with no
disk in it.
Otherwise busybox suppresses them, and the operation fails with an
error instead.
.TP
.B BB_FIX_BACKSLASH
When it is
.BR 1 ,
.B ls
converts backslashes in its operands to forward slashes, so that the
names it prints have forward slashes.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands wildcards in the arguments of
.B busybox
when it starts, which serves commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe .
It expands quoted arguments too, and leaves a pattern that matches
nothing as it is.
When the variable is unset, busybox then sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so that busybox programs it starts, such as from the shell, do not
expand their arguments again.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets that the shell and other applets should not run by name, so
that a program in
.B PATH
runs instead.
It does not affect starting an applet with its launcher or with
.BR busybox .
The value is one of:
.RS
.TP
.B \-
Use no applets.
.TP
.B +
Use a program of the same name in
.BR PATH ,
when there is one, instead of any applet.
.TP
.I list
Applet names separated by spaces, commas, or semicolons.
The names before the first semicolon are not used at all, and those
after it are replaced only when a program of that name is in
.BR PATH .
.RE
.IP
In the kit, the program found is usually the applet's own launcher in
.IR bin ,
which runs the applet anyway, unless a directory earlier in
.B PATH
has a program of that name.
.TP
.B BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
The older form of
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
used only when that is unset:
.B 1
means mode 1,
.B 2
means mode 5, and any other value means mode 0.
.TP
.B BB_SYSTEMROOT
A drive, such as
.BR D: ,
to use instead of the system drive, the drive that holds the Windows
system directory.
A directory works too.
The system drive is where
.B "\-\-install \-u"
installs, where the login shell looks for
.IR /etc/profile ,
and where
.B man
looks for its configuration files in
.IR /etc .
.TP
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
How busybox uses the console, from 0 to 5.
In virtual terminal (VT) output mode, the console interprets the escape
sequences that applets write, such as for color.
Otherwise busybox interprets the common ones itself, through the console
API.
VT input mode makes the console send escape sequences for keys.
.RS
.TP
.B 0
No VT output or input.
.TP
.B 1
Force VT output.
.TP
.B 2
Force VT input.
.TP
.B 3
Force VT output and input.
.TP
.B 4
Use VT output and input only when the console already has them
enabled.
.TP
.B 5
Like 4, but also enable VT output when the console supports it.
.RE
.IP
The default is 5, or 0 when
.B CONEMUPID
is set or under Wine.
A number outside this range means 5, and a value that is not a number
means 0.
Modes 0 to 3 are meant for testing.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal, applied before each applet runs.
The default is
.BR 002 .
The owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
makes new files read-only.
The group and other write bits,
.BR 022 ,
only remove those bits from the permissions that busybox reports.
The shell's
.B umask
command sets and exports this variable.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
The terminal width and height for applets that fit their output to it,
such as
.B ls
and
.BR less ,
and the width of the applet list that
.B busybox
prints with no arguments.
The defaults are the size of the console window, or 80 by 24 when
neither standard output nor standard error is a console.
When set, they override the console window, and a value of 1 or less,
or 30000 or more, means 80 or 24.
.TP
.B HOME
When it is unset, busybox uses the user's profile directory.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides how text is handled.
When its value is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes, and console output is written without code page
conversion.
Otherwise text is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, and single
bytes when it is not.
.TP
.B PATH
Where external programs are found; see
.BR "Running commands" .
.TP
.B TMPDIR
When it is unset, busybox uses
.BR TMP ,
then
.BR TEMP .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, for
.B busybox
with no arguments or with one of the
.BR OPTIONS .
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments to
.B \-\-install
or
.BR \-\-uninstall .
.TP
.B 127
The applet was not found.
.TP
.B 382
A launcher could not start
.IR busybox.exe ,
such as when it is missing from the launcher's directory.
The launcher prints a message beginning
.B w64devkit:
on standard error.
The kit's shell sees it as 126, its low byte.
.PP
Otherwise the exit status is that of the applet.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the applets:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox \-\-list
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the usage of an applet:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox \-\-help sort
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run an applet that has no launcher:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox sha384sum kit.zip
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the applets that the shell runs in its own process:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox \-\-list\-full | grep NOFORK
.EE
.RE
.PP
In the shell, run
.I wget.exe
from
.I ~/bin
instead of the
.B wget
applet:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ export PATH="$HOME/bin;$PATH"
$ export BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS=wget
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Paths may use forward or back slashes.
Prefer forward slashes, since in the shell a backslash quotes the next
character.
An absolute path has a drive, as in
.IR c:/path ,
or is a UNC path, as in
.IR //host/share/path .
A path such as
.I /path
is relative to the root of the current drive.
.PP
A path such as
.I c:name
is relative to the current directory of that drive, and a bare drive such
as
.I c:
is that directory, as in Windows.
Applets name the entries of
.I c:
as
.IR c:name ,
and the root of the drive is
.IR c:/ .
.PP
Applets accept these Unix device names:
.I /dev/null
for
.BR NUL ,
.I /dev/tty
for the console,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.IR /dev/fd/N .
.PP
Files and standard streams are binary, with no CRLF translation:
applets write LF line endings.
.PP
Owners and permissions are synthesized.
A file shows as writable unless it is read-only, and as executable when
its name ends in
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.BR .cmd ,
when it begins with
.BR #! ,
or when it is a Windows program.
.PP
busybox has a manifest that makes its ANSI code page UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 or later, so that file names, arguments, and text may
contain any Unicode character.
On older systems they are limited to the system's ANSI code page.
Console output is converted to the console's code page, unless
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
or
.B LANG
is
.BR C .
If both of the console's code pages are 850 when busybox first uses the
console, it changes them to 858, which adds the euro sign.
.PP
w64devkit changes busybox-w32 as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
The login shell also reads the kit's
.IR etc/profile ,
line editing never beeps, Tab does nothing where a command name is
expected but none is typed, the history holds 1024 lines by default and
up to 16384, and the
.B noiconify
option is on by default, so that
.B noconsole
hides the console window instead of minimizing it; see
.BR sh (1).
.IP \(bu 2
Automatic decompression, as in
.B tar
and
.BR man ,
recognizes zstd data and decompresses it with the kit's
.BR unzstd ,
and
.B tar
has a
.B \-\-zstd
option.
.IP \(bu 2
gzip data is decompressed by running the kit's
.BR gunzip .
.IP \(bu 2
.B man
finds the kit's pages and formats them with the kit's
.BR nroff ,
and
.B less \-R
passes color and font escape sequences through.
